  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,8]?$\n\n- $g(x)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}$\n- $s(x)=(x-2)^{2}$\n- $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x-3}$",
  "worked_solution": "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,8],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,8]$ and differentiable on $(1,8).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}$ is continuous on $[1,8]$ and has a cusp only at $x=0,$ which is outside $[1,8],$ hence it is differentiable on $(1,8).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)=(x-2)^{2}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[1,8]$ and differentiable on $(1,8).$ Therefore, $s(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x-3}$ is not defined at $x=3$, so it is not continuous at $x=3$, which means it is not continuous on $[1,8].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,8].$",
